  "content": {
    "id": "p30-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Does there exist a bijection from $(0,1)$ to $[0,1]$?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Yes.\n\nSend $\\frac12,\\frac13,\\frac14,\\ldots$ to $0,1,\\frac12,\\frac13,\\ldots$, respectively, and leave every other point unchanged.",
      "worked": "Yes. Define $f:(0,1)\\to[0,1]$ by\n$$f(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&x=\\frac12,\\\\1,&x=\\frac13,\\\\\\frac1{n-2},&x=\\frac1n\\text{ for an integer }n\\ge4,\\\\x,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}$$\nThe distinct points $\\frac12,\\frac13,\\frac14,\\ldots$ have distinct images $0,1,\\frac12,\\frac13,\\ldots$. All other points are fixed, and their images are disjoint from these images. Thus $f$ is injective.\n\nThe endpoints $0$ and $1$ have preimages $\\frac12$ and $\\frac13$. Each $\\frac1m$, for an integer $m\\ge2$, has preimage $\\frac1{m+2}$. Every other point of $(0,1)$ is its own preimage. Thus $f$ is surjective, so it is a bijection.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
